<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<ModMetaData>
  <name>Celestial Lighting</name>
  <author>Joof</author>
  <packageId>joof.celestiallighting</packageId>
  <supportedVersions>
    <li>1.6</li>
  </supportedVersions>
  <description>Latitude- and season-aware sky lighting.

- Shadows now lean with the sun's real seasonal position instead of always pointing the same way: the effect grows stronger the further a colony sits from the equator, and mirrors between the northern and southern hemispheres.
- Sunrise and sunset last longer and glow warmer at high latitudes, matching the long, colorful twilight seen at real-world high latitudes. Colonies near the equator see little change.
- Shadows also stretch very slightly longer on the side of the map the sun is leaning away from, subtly following the same seasonal lean.</description>
  <modDependencies>
    <li>
      <packageId>brrainz.harmony</packageId>
      <displayName>Harmony</displayName>
      <steamWorkshopUrl>steam://url/CommunityFilePage/2009463077</steamWorkshopUrl>
    </li>
  </modDependencies>
  <!-- Perspective: Eaves (Owlchemist, continued by Mlie) does one thing we now do ourselves — make
       roofed-but-unenclosed cells cast shadows — and it does it by transpiling
       SectionLayer_SunShadows.Regenerate. Patch_ShadowMeshPerimeter replaces that whole method with
       a Prefix (it has to: §4 fills in a shadow face vanilla never builds, and Harmony cannot append
       to an already-finalized mesh), so its transpiled body never executes. Load order cannot fix
       that — one of the two mods is always dead. Rather than leave it silently half-working (its
       indoor-mask and rain-fog patches keep running while its shadows quietly do nothing), we
       declare it outright and reimplement the feature natively as §15: same UsesOutdoorTemperature
       rule, resolved per section instead of per map, and toggleable under "Eave shadows". -->
  <incompatibleWith>
    <!-- "Perspective: Eaves (Continued)", the 1.6-current fork this was verified against. -->
    <li>Mlie.PerspectiveEaves</li>
    <!-- Owlchemist's original, superseded but still installed in plenty of mod lists. Same
         transpiler, same collision. -->
    <li>Owlchemist.PerspectiveEaves</li>
  </incompatibleWith>
  <loadAfter>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld</li>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld.Royalty</li>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld.Ideology</li>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld.Biotech</li>
    <li>Ludeon.RimWorld.Anomaly</li>
    <li>brrainz.harmony</li>
    <!-- Two separate interops with Dub's Skylights, one of them a genuine shared patch target:
         (a) it reads SkyManager.CurSkyGlow, which only our opt-in accessibility minimum-brightness
         floor writes (via the canonical ForceSetCurSkyGlow setter), so it sees the same lifted glow
         for skylit rooms — the intended composition, not a conflict; and (b) §7b's
         Patch_IndoorSkyOcclusion shares SectionLayer_LightingOverlay.Regenerate with it. Skylights
         brackets that method — its Prefix nulls map.roofGrid for skylit cells so vanilla's roofed
         branch never fires, its Postfix restores them — and we are a Postfix taking Priority.First so
         we run BEFORE that restore and therefore read skylit cells as unroofed, leaving them sky-lit.
         Loading after it is what makes that ordering hold. -->
    <li>Dubwise.DubsSkylights</li>
    <!-- Shared patch target with opposite intent, declared so the ordering is at least deterministic:
         Biomes! Caverns transpiles SectionLayer_LightingOverlay.Regenerate to make cavern roofs read
         as unroofed (its caverns are meant to feel sky-lit), while §7b raises sky occlusion for roofed
         cells. Our Postfix only ever RAISES the baked alpha, so it can add occlusion to a body that mod
         has already transpiled without corrupting it — but the two features do want opposite things
         about caverns specifically. With both installed, "Black unlit interiors" is the toggle to turn
         off if you want Biomes! Caverns' look. -->
    <li>BiomesTeam.BiomesCaverns</li>
    <!-- Soft compat: Vanilla Races Expanded – Sanguophage owns the VRE_BloodMoonCondition
         GameCondition we recolour the night around (see Patch_BloodMoon / DESIGN.md §12). We take
         no hard reference to it — the effect is inert when it's absent — but load after it so our
         render reads its condition state once it has started applying it. -->
    <li>vanillaracesexpanded.sanguophage</li>
  </loadAfter>
</ModMetaData>
